# promptforge writes its artifacts verbatim — emit.ts calls writeFileSync with
# no line-ending conversion — so a build inherits the platform's endings: CRLF
# on a Windows checkout, LF on Linux. CI rebuilds on ubuntu and fails the
# "Generated files match the committed ones" step on any drift, so a generated
# file committed from Windows can never match what the runner produces. A
# contributor who converts the files by hand then watches the next local build
# put the CRLF straight back, before they can stage it.
#
# Normalising at the git layer ends that loop and is the only fix the emitter
# does not undo: the working tree keeps whatever the local build wrote, and the
# index always stores LF — which is what the runner produces and compares
# against. Forcing LF inside the emitter would be wrong instead, since the
# generated files would then disagree with the CRLF source templates already
# committed, breaking skills.test.ts for every Windows developer.
#
# Scoped to the emitted artifacts and their sources rather than declared
# repo-wide, so this cannot reformat anything it was not aimed at.
*.md text eol=lf
*.md.tmpl text eol=lf
*.generated.ts text eol=lf
